What is Maltodextrin?
Maltodextrin is a polysaccharide widely used as a food additive, derived from starches such as corn, rice, potato, wheat, and tapioca through partial hydrolysis with a dextrose equivalent (DE) below 20%. It typically appears as a white hygroscopic spray-dried powder. Maltodextrin serves as an essential ingredient across multiple industries due to its excellent solubility, neutral flavor profile, and ability to enhance texture and stability in various formulations.

